          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Guides",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Concrete Popping Out",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/concrete-popping-out/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What causes small holes to pop out of concrete?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most small cone-shaped holes are popouts caused by porous or reactive stones near the surface, such as chert or some shales, which absorb water and expand when frozen or through alkali-silica reaction. The expansion breaks off a cone of concrete above the stone. You can usually see the split stone at the bottom of each hole."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are concrete popouts a structural problem?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Generally no. Industry references describe popouts as surface defects that do not affect the structural capacity of a slab. They are cosmetic and can collect water, but they do not mean the concrete is failing. Popouts often slow down after the first few winters as the near-surface stones that are prone to it pop out."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between concrete spalling and scaling?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Scaling is a thin, broad flaking of the surface, usually 1/8 to 1/4 in deep, often from freeze-thaw and deicers. Spalling is deeper and more localized, with larger chunks breaking off along edges, joints or over corroding steel. People often use spalling for both. The depth and pattern point to the cause."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you fix concrete that is popping out?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Clean popouts and fill them with a polymer-modified patch or a small epoxy patch. Widespread scaling is better handled with a resurfacer over the whole slab. Edge spalls need square-cut edges and a repair mortar. Corrosion spalls need the steel cleaned and primed before patching. Match the fix to the cause."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will sealing concrete stop popouts?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It reduces them but cannot guarantee none. A penetrating silane or siloxane sealer lowers how much water reaches the surface stones and paste, which cuts freeze-thaw damage and chloride penetration. Stones that are already saturated or reactive may still pop. Sealing is most valuable on newer slabs and in climates with frequent freeze-thaw cycles."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it the contractor's fault if new concrete is scaling?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Sometimes. Scaling in the first year or two can come from too little entrained air, finishing while bleed water was present, steel troweling exterior concrete, or poor curing. It can also come from deicers used in the first winter. Photograph the damage, note whether salt was used, and contact the installer while any warranty is valid."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I replace concrete that is spalling?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not usually for popouts or limited spalls; patching or resurfacing is far cheaper. Consider replacement when scaling covers most of the slab and goes deeper than about 1/4 in in many places, when patches keep failing, when the slab is also cracked and settled, or when corrosion is widespread. A resurfacer cannot save a surface that keeps deteriorating."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
