        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          When concrete starts popping out, the instinct is to blame the concrete as a whole and assume the slab is failing. Usually it is not. Concrete popouts, surface scaling and spalling look similar from the porch but have different causes: a bad stone in the mix, water freezing in the surface, deicing salts, a finisher who worked bleed water back into the top, or steel rusting underneath. Each one has its own repair, and two of them are mostly cosmetic. Look closely at the holes before you buy a patch, because the cause decides whether a repair lasts a decade or one winter.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="shape-of-damage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Read the Shape of the Damage First
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Popouts</h3>

          <p>
            Popouts are small conical holes, typically from about 1/4 in to 2 in across, scattered over the surface. At the bottom of each hole you can usually see a broken piece of stone, often split in half, with the other half stuck in the cone that popped off. They tend to show up after the first winter or two and then slow down, because the bad stones near the surface eventually all pop.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Scaling</h3>

          <p>
            Scaling is a thin layer of the surface flaking off over a broad area, usually 1/8 to 1/4 in deep, exposing the coarse aggregate underneath so the slab looks like rough gravel. It typically starts where water and deicer sit, such as tire tracks, the bottom of a slope, or the walk near the door, and spreads from there.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Spalling</h3>

          <p>
            Spalling is the general word for chunks breaking off, and people use it loosely for scaling too. Strictly, spalls are deeper and larger than scaling, often along edges and joints or over buried steel, with irregular broken faces rather than a uniform peeled layer. A spall over a rust stain, or a crack running in a straight line where rebar or mesh would be, is a corrosion spall. The <a href="/concrete-crack-repair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete crack repair</a> guide covers how corrosion cracks start.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Write Down What You See</h3>

          <p>
            Write down where the damage is, how deep, and what is at the bottom of the holes. Stone in the bottom of small cones points to popouts. Even shallow flaking in high-salt areas points to scaling. Deep, irregular pieces off edges or over rust points to spalling or corrosion. That ten-minute inspection decides everything below.
          </p>

