        <div id="popouts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Concrete Popouts From Absorptive Aggregate
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Absorptive Stones That Swell</h3>

          <p>
            Classic concrete popouts come from individual pieces of aggregate that soak up water and expand. Chert, some shales, porous limestone and lightweight particles such as lignite or iron-bearing stones are common culprits, depending on where the gravel was mined. When a porous stone sits close to the surface, water fills it, freezes, and the expansion breaks off the cone of mortar above it. Chert popouts are a familiar complaint in parts of the Midwest where local gravel contains it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Alkali-Silica Reaction</h3>

          <p>
            Some popouts are caused by alkali-silica reaction instead: certain reactive silica in the aggregate reacts with alkalis in the cement and forms a gel that swells with water. Those can occur without freezing and sometimes show a glassy or gel-like residue in the hole. Either way the mechanism is the same from the homeowner's view: a specific stone near the surface expanded and broke out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Popouts Are Almost Always Cosmetic</h3>

          <p>
            The reassuring part is that popouts are almost always cosmetic. The Portland Cement Association and most concrete industry references describe them as surface defects that do not affect the structural capacity of a slab. They are annoying, they collect water and dirt, and on a decorative surface they look bad, but a driveway with popouts is not a driveway that is failing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">They Usually Taper Off</h3>

          <p>
            The other good news is that popouts usually taper off. The weak stones closest to the surface pop in the first few winters, and deeper ones are protected by more concrete. If a slab has a burst of popouts in years one through three and then mostly stops, that is typical. If it keeps getting worse year after year and the holes are joined by flaking, you are likely looking at scaling as well.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Popouts are cosmetic. Patch them for looks, seal the slab to slow new ones.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A popout is one bad stone, not a failing slab. Repair the holes that bother you, then use a penetrating sealer to cut how much water reaches the stones that have not popped yet.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="freeze-thaw" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Freeze-Thaw and Deicers: Scaling and Spalling
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Freezing Damages the Surface</h3>

          <p>
            Water expands roughly 9 percent when it freezes. Concrete is porous, and if the surface paste is saturated when it freezes, that expansion creates pressure in the pores. Concrete for freeze-thaw climates is supposed to be air-entrained, meaning an admixture creates billions of tiny bubbles that give the freezing water room to expand. Slabs poured without enough entrained air, or with air knocked out by over-finishing, scale badly in their first few winters.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Deicers Make It Worse</h3>

          <p>
            Deicers make it worse in two ways. Salts keep the surface wetter and increase the number of freeze-thaw cycles, because melted water refreezes at night. And some chemicals attack the concrete directly: magnesium chloride and especially ammonium-based products such as ammonium nitrate and ammonium sulfate are widely cited as damaging to concrete paste. Our guide to <a href="/does-salt-damage-concrete/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether salt damages concrete</a> goes through the chemistry, and <a href="/best-ice-melt-for-concrete/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">our ice melt picks for concrete</a> cover safer options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Young Concrete Is Most Vulnerable</h3>

          <p>
            Young concrete is the most vulnerable. Industry guidance commonly says not to use any deicer on concrete during its first winter, and many recommend keeping salts off for the first year. The surface is still gaining strength, and it has not had time to dry out enough to resist saturation. A lot of scaling on new driveways traces back to a bag of rock salt in the first January.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Salt Arrives on Tires</h3>

          <p>
            Salt also arrives on tires. Road brine dripping off cars parked on a driveway or garage floor delivers concentrated chloride right where tires sit, which is why scaling often follows tire tracks even when the homeowner never uses salt. Rinsing those areas during thaws and sealing the slab are the practical defenses. See <a href="/how-to-prevent-concrete-spalling-in-winter/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to prevent concrete spalling in winter</a> for the full routine.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Freeze-Thaw Reference Figures</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">9%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Approximate expansion of water as it freezes</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1 year</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common no-deicer window for new concrete</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">4-7%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical entrained air target for exterior concrete</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1/8 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Minimum depth many resurfacers are rated for</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="finishing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Overworked Finishes and Weak Surfaces
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Finishing With Bleed Water Present</h3>

          <p>
            After concrete is placed, water rises to the surface. This bleed water is normal, and finishers are supposed to wait for it to evaporate before final floating and troweling. When a finisher works the surface while bleed water is present, or sprinkles water on to make troweling easier, that water gets mixed back into the top layer. The top 1/8 in or so ends up with a much higher water-to-cement ratio than the rest of the slab, which makes it weak and porous.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Overworking and Hard Troweling</h3>

          <p>
            Overworking also drives entrained air out of the surface, and hard steel troweling seals the top so tightly on exterior slabs that water cannot escape. Exterior concrete should normally get a broom finish after floating, not a slick steel-troweled finish. A shiny exterior slab is a warning sign for future scaling, especially in a freeze-thaw climate.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Poor Curing</h3>

          <p>
            Poor curing finishes the job. Concrete needs moisture for days to develop its strength, and a surface that dries out in hot sun and wind in the first day never reaches full strength. That weak skin is what flakes off first. Many contractors apply a curing compound or cover the slab with wet burlap or plastic to prevent this, and the difference shows up years later in how well the surface holds.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Finishing Damage Shows Up</h3>

          <p>
            Finishing-related scaling usually shows up evenly across a slab, or across one pour, rather than only in salty spots. If one section of a driveway poured on the same day as another is scaling and the other is not, finishing or a different truckload is the likely difference. That pattern matters if the slab is new enough to be under a contractor warranty, so photograph it and contact the installer before you patch anything.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rebar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Spalling From Rusting Rebar and Mesh
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Embedded Steel Rusts</h3>

          <p>
            Steel in concrete is normally protected because the concrete is highly alkaline, which forms a passive layer on the steel. When chlorides from deicers or seawater reach the steel, or when the concrete carbonates over decades, that protection breaks down and the steel rusts. Rust occupies several times the volume of the original steel, and that expansion cracks and pops off the concrete over it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shallow Cover Is the Biggest Risk</h3>

          <p>
            The biggest risk factor is shallow cover. Welded wire mesh that ended up lying on the ground during the pour, or rebar sitting an inch from the surface instead of near mid-depth, rusts far sooner. Corrosion spalls often show as straight cracks that follow the grid of the steel, rust staining, and a hollow sound when you tap near the crack. Eventually a piece lifts off and you can see the rusted bar.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The One Progressive Form of Damage</h3>

          <p>
            This is the one form of concrete popping out that is genuinely progressive. Patching over a rusting bar without dealing with the steel just traps the corrosion, and the patch usually spalls off within a few years, sometimes with a ring of new damage around it. On steps, balconies, retaining walls and anything structural, corrosion spalling deserves a professional assessment.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Proper Steel Repair</h3>

          <p>
            The proper repair is to cut out the concrete around the corroded steel, clean the bar to bright metal, assess whether it has lost too much section, coat it with a corrosion-inhibiting primer, and rebuild with a repair mortar. That is doable for a homeowner on a small spall on a step, but a large area or a load-bearing element is a contractor job. Our <a href="/foundation-crack-repair-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">foundation crack repair cost</a> guide gives a sense of professional pricing for related work.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repair-by-cause" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Repair by Cause: Patching Popouts and Spalls
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Scattered Popouts</h3>

          <p>
            For scattered popouts, clean each hole with a wire brush and vacuum, remove any loose remnant of the stone, and fill it with a polymer-modified patch or a fast-setting repair mortar. Dampen the hole first so it does not suck water out of the patch. Overfill slightly and strike flush, then broom or sponge to match the texture around it. Small epoxy patch kits also work well in popouts and set hard enough to resist tires. Our <a href="/concrete-repair-products/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete repair products</a> guide sorts these by damage type.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Widespread Scaling</h3>

          <p>
            For widespread scaling, individual patches look like a quilt. A concrete resurfacer, which is a polymer-modified cement topping applied at about 1/8 in or more, gives the whole slab a uniform new surface. Prep is everything: pressure-wash at high pressure to remove every loose flake, fill deep spots and cracks first, keep the slab saturated surface-dry, and work in sections between control joints. Our <a href="/best-concrete-resurfacer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best concrete resurfacer</a> guide covers product choices.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Edge and Joint Spalls</h3>

          <p>
            For edge and joint spalls, saw-cut or chisel the edges of the damaged area square to at least 1/2 in deep so the patch does not feather to nothing at the edges, where it would break off. Use a repair mortar rated for the depth, and if the spall runs along a control joint, keep the joint open by placing a strip of foam or a piece of cardboard in it while you patch, then seal the joint with flexible sealant after the patch cures.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Corrosion Spalls and Knowing When to Stop</h3>

          <p>
            For corrosion spalls, follow the steel-repair sequence above. Sealing the crack over a rusting bar is not a repair. It is worth checking whether you have a <a href="/concrete-repair-kit/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete repair kit</a> that includes a bonding agent and a structural repair mortar, but on anything holding weight, have a pro look first.
          </p>

          <p>
            And know when patching is not worth it. When scaling covers most of a driveway and is deeper than about 1/4 in in many places, or when spalls keep returning around old patches, resurfacing is the minimum, and replacement can be the better value. A resurfacer bonded to a weak, still-deteriorating surface will fail with it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prevention" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Preventing Concrete Popouts and Spalling
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Seal Exterior Concrete</h3>

          <p>
            Seal exterior concrete in freeze-thaw climates. A silane or siloxane penetrating sealer reduces how much water the surface absorbs without forming a slippery film, and less water in the pores means less freeze damage and less chloride reaching steel. Most penetrating sealers are rated to last several years between applications. See <a href="/best-penetrating-concrete-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">our penetrating concrete sealer picks</a> and <a href="/how-to-seal-a-concrete-driveway/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to seal a concrete driveway</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Control Deicers</h3>

          <p>
            Control deicers. Shovel early so less ice forms, use sand for traction where you can, avoid ammonium-based products completely, and use the smallest effective amount of a gentler deicer. Keep all deicers off concrete in its first winter. Rinse salt residue during thaws, especially where cars park.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Specify New Concrete Properly</h3>

          <p>
            If you are pouring new concrete, the specification is your best prevention. Ask for air-entrained concrete in freeze-thaw climates, a strength suited to exterior flatwork, and aggregate from a source without a history of popouts; local ready-mix suppliers know which pits have chert problems. Ask the finisher to wait for bleed water, use a broom finish, and cure properly. Ask that mesh or rebar be supported on chairs at mid-depth, not left on the ground.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep Water Moving Off the Slab</h3>

          <p>
            Finally, keep water moving off the slab. Concrete that drains dries quickly and spends less time saturated, which is the condition freeze-thaw damage needs. Regrade low spots next to the slab, keep downspouts from discharging onto it, and fill cracks and joints with flexible sealant so water does not soak the base and the edges. Prevention is cheap compared with resurfacing, and it is the only thing that slows every one of the causes on this page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What causes small holes to pop out of concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most small cone-shaped holes are popouts caused by porous or reactive stones near the surface, such as chert or some shales, which absorb water and expand when frozen or through alkali-silica reaction. The expansion breaks off a cone of concrete above the stone. You can usually see the split stone at the bottom of each hole.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are concrete popouts a structural problem?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally no. Industry references describe popouts as surface defects that do not affect the structural capacity of a slab. They are cosmetic and can collect water, but they do not mean the concrete is failing. Popouts often slow down after the first few winters as the near-surface stones that are prone to it pop out.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between concrete spalling and scaling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Scaling is a thin, broad flaking of the surface, usually 1/8 to 1/4 in deep, often from freeze-thaw and deicers. Spalling is deeper and more localized, with larger chunks breaking off along edges, joints or over corroding steel. People often use spalling for both. The depth and pattern point to the cause.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you fix concrete that is popping out?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Clean popouts and fill them with a polymer-modified patch or a small epoxy patch. Widespread scaling is better handled with a resurfacer over the whole slab. Edge spalls need square-cut edges and a repair mortar. Corrosion spalls need the steel cleaned and primed before patching. Match the fix to the cause.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will sealing concrete stop popouts?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It reduces them but cannot guarantee none. A penetrating silane or siloxane sealer lowers how much water reaches the surface stones and paste, which cuts freeze-thaw damage and chloride penetration. Stones that are already saturated or reactive may still pop. Sealing is most valuable on newer slabs and in climates with frequent freeze-thaw cycles.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it the contractor's fault if new concrete is scaling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sometimes. Scaling in the first year or two can come from too little entrained air, finishing while bleed water was present, steel troweling exterior concrete, or poor curing. It can also come from deicers used in the first winter. Photograph the damage, note whether salt was used, and contact the installer while any warranty is valid.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I replace concrete that is spalling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not usually for popouts or limited spalls; patching or resurfacing is far cheaper. Consider replacement when scaling covers most of the slab and goes deeper than about 1/4 in in many places, when patches keep failing, when the slab is also cracked and settled, or when corrosion is widespread. A resurfacer cannot save a surface that keeps deteriorating.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating popouts as a failing slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Scattered conical holes with a split stone at the bottom are aggregate popouts. They are cosmetic and usually taper off after the first few winters. Replacing a driveway over popouts wastes thousands. Patch the holes that bother you, seal the surface, and keep an eye out for flaking, which is a different problem.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Feathering patches to zero thickness</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cement patches spread thin at the edges break off within a season, leaving a ring of new damage. Cut or chisel the edges of a spall square to at least 1/2 in deep, or use a resurfacer rated for thin application. Respect the minimum depth on the bag.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Patching over rusting rebar</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A patch over corroding steel traps the rust, and the expanding steel pops the patch and more concrete around it. Expose the bar, clean it to bright metal, prime it with a corrosion inhibitor, then rebuild. On structural elements, get a professional opinion first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Salting new concrete the first winter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Concrete is still gaining strength and drying in its first year, and deicers combined with freeze-thaw cycles are the most common cause of early scaling. Use sand for traction instead. One bag of rock salt in the first January can cost a whole new surface.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Resurfacing without proper prep</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A resurfacer only bonds as well as the surface beneath it. Loose flakes, dirt, sealer and weak paste must come off with a high-pressure wash or grinding, and the slab must be saturated surface-dry at application. Skipping prep makes the new layer delaminate along with the old surface.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

