        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Concrete popping out is usually one of five things: a bad stone near the surface, freeze-thaw scaling, deicer damage, a weak overworked finish, or steel rusting underneath. The first is cosmetic, the middle three are surface problems a resurfacer and better habits can handle, and only the last is genuinely progressive.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Look at the holes, match the repair to the cause, and then seal the slab and control deicers so the next winter does less damage. Patching without changing what caused the damage just schedules the same repair for next spring.
          </p>
        </div>

